 for visualization we can integrade open-telemetry with prometheus and grafana.



You used synchronized to fix race conditions but performance dropped —
how else could you fix the issue?
Example: Use ConcurrentHashMap Instead of synchronized Map
Map<String, Object> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

For counters or accumulators:
AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger();
counter.incrementAndGet();

 2. Use ReentrantLock (More Flexible Than synchronized)
Gives you:

Try-locks

Fairness policy

Explicit lock/unlock
private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

public void doSomething() {
    if (lock.tryLock()) {
        try {
            // critical section
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    } else {
        // fallback or skip
    }
}

| Use Case              | Better Than `synchronized`                  |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Shared Map/List       | `ConcurrentHashMap`, `CopyOnWriteArrayList` |
| Shared counter        | `AtomicInteger`, `LongAdder`                |
| Heavy read/low write  | `ReadWriteLock`, `StampedLock`              |
| Queued processing     | `BlockingQueue`, actor pattern              |
| Thread-specific data  | `ThreadLocal`                               |
| Complex lock behavior | `ReentrantLock`                             |

In a producer-consumer setup, consumers are waiting infinitely —
how do you debug this?
When consumers wait infinitely in a producer-consumer setup,
it usually means something is wrong with signaling or shared state management —
 producers may not be notifying, or the queue is empty and stuck

1:BlockingQueue<String> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(10);
In Java concurrency, a BlockingQueue is an interface within the
java.util.concurrent package that extends the Queue interface.
It provides a thread-safe mechanism for handling shared data between threads,
particularly in producer-consumer scenarios.

The defining feature of a BlockingQueue is its "blocking" behavior.
If a thread attempts to add an element to a full BlockingQueue, the put()
method will block the thread until space becomes available in the queue.
If a thread attempts to retrieve an element from an empty BlockingQueue,
 the take() method will block the thread until an element becomes available
 in the queue.

2:a:Is the Producer Actually Producing?
Log every production: "Produced item: X"
Ensure the producer thread is still running
If using a bounded queue: is it full and blocking?

b. Is the Queue Empty?
If using a BlockingQueue, consumers will block on .take() if the queue is empty.
Use logs or debugger to check:

c:check wait & notify() is written properly

d:use thread dump to check further

| Queue Type                           | Potential Issue                                  | Fix                                           |
| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------- |
| `BlockingQueue`                      | Empty, no producer                               | Ensure producer is running and putting        |
| Custom queue with `wait/notify`      | Missed `notify()`, or not in loop                | Use `while`, always `notifyAll()`             |
| Bounded queue (`ArrayBlockingQueue`) | Producer blocked on full queue                   | Add logging, increase size, or consume faster |
| Asynchronous setup (e.g., Kafka)     | Wrong topic, partition, or consumer group config | Check offsets and logs                        |

You used ThreadPoolExecutor, but it silently discards tasks —
what went wrong in the config
If your ThreadPoolExecutor is silently discarding tasks, it's likely
due to a misconfigured RejectedExecutionHandler, especially the default one.

| Policy                | Behavior                                                    |
| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| `AbortPolicy`         | ❌ Throws exception (default)                                |
| `DiscardPolicy`       | ❌ Silently discards                                         |
| `DiscardOldestPolicy` | ❌ Removes oldest, submits new task (can starve older tasks) |
| `CallerRunsPolicy`    | ✅ Runs task in calling thread (natural throttling)          |

ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
    10, 20, 60L, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
    new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(100),
    Executors.defaultThreadFactory(),
    new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy()  // backpressure instead of silent discard
);

@Configuration
public class ExecutorConfig {

    @Bean(name = "taskExecutor")
    public ThreadPoolTaskExecutor taskExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();

        executor.setCorePoolSize(10);             // core threads
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(20);              // max threads
        executor.setQueueCapacity(100);           // task queue size
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("MyAppExec-");
        executor.setKeepAliveSeconds(60);         // optional

        // Safe rejection policy — applies backpressure
        executor.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());

        // OR: Log rejections instead of silently discarding
        // executor.setRejectedExecutionHandler((r, e) -> {
        //     System.err.println("Rejected task: " + r.toString());
        // });

        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }
}
